::CW_U01_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "The candle trembled in the window." Which craft term best describes the word "trembled"?{
=A. Diction#Correct. "Trembled" is a word choice that shapes the effect of the sentence.
~B. Plot#Incorrect. Plot is the sequence of events, not a single word choice.
~C. Dialogue#Incorrect. No character is speaking in the passage.
~D. Setting#Incorrect. The window is part of setting, but "trembled" is diction.
}
::CW_U01_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The hallway exhaled the last of the students into the rain." What connotation does "exhaled" create?{
~A. Loud excitement#Incorrect. "Exhaled" suggests release, not loud excitement.
=B. Tired release#Correct. "Exhaled" makes the hallway seem as if it is breathing out after a long day.
~C. Formal celebration#Incorrect. The word does not suggest ceremony or celebration.
~D. Careless humor#Incorrect. The word creates weariness more than comedy.
}
::CW_U01_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "One step. Then another. Then the door." What tone does the syntax help create?{
~A. Relaxed#Incorrect. The fragments create pressure rather than relaxation.
~B. Cheerful#Incorrect. The sentence pattern does not create happiness.
=C. Suspenseful#Correct. The short fragments slow the moment and build suspense.
~D. Informational#Incorrect. The passage is crafted for dramatic effect, not neutral information.
}
::CW_U01_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which response uses craft vocabulary most precisely?{
~A. The sentence is good because it sounds good.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not name a craft choice.
~B. The writer did a lot of things.#Incorrect. This does not identify any specific technique.
~C. The words are nice and interesting.#Incorrect. This is too general for craft discussion.
=D. The visual imagery of "orange dust" makes the room feel neglected.#Correct. This names imagery, cites words, and explains effect.
}
::CW_U01_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The dog crept toward the porch." Which word has the strongest cautious connotation?{
~A. dog#Incorrect. "Dog" names the subject but does not create the cautious effect.
=B. crept#Correct. "Crept" suggests careful or fearful movement.
~C. toward#Incorrect. "Toward" shows direction, not cautious movement.
~D. porch#Incorrect. "Porch" names a place.
}
